                "text": "Let me understand the rule. Input has single pixels of different colors in two horizontal lines along left and right sides. Output: copy input. If two blocks across from each other are same color, fill the rest of that horizontal row with that color. Don't do anything with different colors.\n\nWait, but the verifier program does more. Let me analyze.\n\nThe verifier: x0 = mostcolor(I) - most common color (0). x1 = asobject(I) - all cells. x4 = sfilter removing cells with most common color, i.e., all non-background pixels. These are the colored pixels on left/right.\n\nx5 = apply initset - each becomes singleton object. x6 = product(x5,x5) - all pairs. x10 = pairs where colors equal. x17 = pairs where either leftmost equal OR uppermost equal. Since all non-background pixels are in left/right columns, pairs with same row (uppermost equal) are the \"across from each other\" pairs. x18 = same-color pairs in same row. x23 = recolor with connect between centers -> fills the row between them.\n\nx37 = mapply x23 x18 = union of all these lines. x38 = paint(I, x37).\n\nThen x36: x34 = toindices(x4) (all non-background cells). x35 = sfilter(x34, x29) where x29 checks if last (column) is 0 or width-1. So x35 = non-background cells on borders. x36 = equality(x34, x35). If all non-bg pixels are on left/right borders (columns 0 or w-1), then x36 is true.\n\nThen x39 = branch(x36, x29, x33). x29 checks column is 0 or w-1. x33 checks row is 0 or h-1.\n\nx40 = all indices, x41 = sfilter(indices, x39), x42 = toobject(x41, I). x43 = paint(x38, x42).\n\nSo if all non-background pixels are on left/right columns, then x39 = x29 (border columns), so x41 = all cells in columns 0 and w-1. x42 = object of those cells with their values from I. Paint them into x38 - which just repaints same values. So no change.\n\nIf not (some non-bg pixels not on left/right borders), x39 = x33 = border rows, x41 = all cells in rows 0 and h-1, x42 = values... paint. That would clear? Actually paint sets those cells to their input values, no change.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually the grid I passed has border rows all zeros, so painting them changes nothing.\n\nSo the verifier essentially: for each row where the left pixel and right pixel have the same color, fill entire row with that color. Actually pair across means same row (uppermost equal). But also x17 includes leftmost equal (same column). If two pixels in same column with same color... left/right columns only have one pixel per row typically. But if there are multiple pixels in same column with same color, it would draw a vertical line. Hmm, in this example only single pixels per side.\n\nActually x17 = either leftmost equal or uppermost equal. connect between centers draws a line. For same row, horizontal line fills the row. For same column, vertical line.\n\nBut in these tasks, we just need to produce 3 demonstration pairs that capture the rule. The Student sees only inputs and outputs. We want to teach a clear rule.\n\nThe rule: rows where the two side pixels are same color get fully filled with that color.\n\nLet me create 3 examples. Grid sizes can vary. Let me use consistent dimensions with side pixels on columns 0 and w-1.\n\nI need to be careful: the verifier's full behavior. It fills rows where left/right pixels match. Let me just design inputs where that's clear.\n\nLet me make 3 grids of size maybe 7x7 or 9x9.\n\nExample 1: 5x5 grid.\nRow0: all 0.\nRow1: [3,0,0,0,3] -> fill row1 with 3.\nRow2: [4,0,0,0,7] -> different, unchanged.\nRow3: all 0.\nRow4: all 0.\n\nOutput:\nRow1: [3,3,3,3,3].\n\nLet me make examples with varied sizes and colors.\n\nLet me consider the verifier more carefully for what happens with background cells. x4 = non-background. x29 checks border column. x36 equality of x34 (all non-bg indices) and x35 (non-bg on border columns). So as long as all non-bg pixels are in columns 0 or w-1, x36 true, no extra painting. Good, our inputs satisfy this.\n\nThen x38 = input with